<!-- ---------------- ABSTRACT ---------------- -->

<h1><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-font-kerni=
ng:
18.0pt'>Abstract<o:p></o:p></span></h1>

<p>Real estate companies in developing regions, including Somalia, still re=
ly
heavily on manual, paper-based, and spreadsheet-driven methods to manage
construction projects, property inventories, sales, rentals, and payment re=
cords.
These manual approaches are slow, prone to human error, difficult to audit,=
 and
offer management no real-time visibility into business performance. This st=
udy
set out to design and implement a web-based Real Estate Project Management
System capable of digitising and centralising these core operations for a s=
mall
to medium-sized real estate company. The specific objectives were to analyse
the shortcomings of the existing manual system, design a relational database
and role-based system architecture capable of supporting multiple categorie=
s of
staff, implement a functional web application covering project, property,
customer, sales, rental, payment, document, and notification management, and
evaluate the resulting system through structured testing. An iterative,
Agile-inspired methodology was adopted, in which requirements were gathered
through informal interviews and observation of real estate office practices,
followed by successive design, implementation, and testing cycles. The syst=
em
was built using ASP.NET Core 9 MVC for the presentation and application lay=
ers,
Entity Framework Core 9 with a Code-First approach for data access, and
Microsoft SQL Server as the relational database engine, with ASP.NET Core
Identity providing authentication and role-based authorisation for five
distinct user roles. The completed system automatically updates property
availability when a sale or rental is recorded, recalculates outstanding
balances whenever a payment is registered or removed, and raises notificati=
ons for
pending payments, contracts nearing expiry, and delayed projects. Unit-level
verification of the business rules and user-acceptance testing carried out =
with
the demonstration accounts confirmed that all core workflows performed as
designed, indicating a marked improvement in accuracy, speed, and traceabil=
ity
compared with the manual process it replaces. The study concludes that a
moderately-scoped, role-aware web application can materially improve the
operational efficiency of a small real estate business, and it recommends
further work on mobile access, SMS and e-mail notification channels, and
multi-branch support.<o:p></o:p></p>

