<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>4.4 Data flow=
 and
entity relationship diagram<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>At a data flow level, a new property is created within the context of an
existing project; when a sale or a rental is subsequently recorded against =
that
property, the property's status is updated automatically and an initial pay=
ment
is generated if a deposit was specified; every additional payment recorded
against that sale or rental triggers a recalculation of the outstanding bal=
ance
and the corresponding payment status. Figure 4.3 presents the simplified en=
tity
relationship diagram that models these flows at the database level. Each pr=
oject
may contain many properties and many project tasks, and is optionally
supervised by one application user acting as project manager. Each property=
 may
be associated with, at most, one active sale and one active rental at any g=
iven
time, reflecting the business rule that a single property cannot be sold and
rented simultaneously. Each sale and each rental may accumulate many payment
records over time, and each customer may be linked to many sales, rentals, =
and
payments across the lifetime of the system. Documents and notifications are
attached as supporting entities linked, respectively, to the relevant
transactional record and to the application user who should receive the
notification.</p>

