<p>The Projects module allows Administrators and Project Managers to create=
 a project
with a name, code, location, budget, and schedule, and to monitor its statu=
s,
which ranges from Planning through InProgress to Completed, OnHold, or
Cancelled, along with a numeric progress percentage. The Project Tasks modu=
le
allows individual tasks within a project to be assigned to a specific emplo=
yee,
given a priority level and a due date, and tracked through Pending, InProgr=
ess,
Completed, or Delayed states.</p>

<p>The Properties module allows staff to register individual units, of type
Apartment, Villa, Office, Shop, or Land, under a specific project, upload o=
ne
or more images for each unit, and track each unit's status as it moves from
Available or UnderConstruction through to Sold or Rented. The Customers mod=
ule
maintains a directory of buyers and tenants, optionally linked to a
self-service login account, which the Customer role uses to view personal
transaction history.</p>

<p>The Sales and Rentals modules are implemented through the SalesService a=
nd
RentalService classes respectively, both of which enforce the rule that a
property already marked Sold or Rented cannot be sold or rented again. As
illustrated in the code excerpt reproduced in Appendix A, creating a sale
automatically changes the associated property's status to Sold and, where a
deposit was specified, immediately creates a corresponding Payment record;
deleting a sale reverses this by releasing the property back to Available
status and removing any payments linked to that sale.</p>

<p>The Payments module, implemented through PaymentService, is deliberately
kept independent of the SalesService and RentalService classes with respect=
 to
balance calculation: whenever a payment is created or deleted, PaymentServi=
ce
calls a dedicated recalculation method on the relevant sales or rental serv=
ice,
which sums all payments linked to that sale or rental and updates the remai=
ning
balance and payment status accordingly, rather than trusting the calling co=
de
to keep these values consistent. This design decision, referenced earlier in
Section 3.4, ensures that the payment status of every sale and rental is al=
ways
derived directly from its actual payment history.</p>

<p>The Documents module allows a file, such as a signed sales contract or a
payment receipt, to be uploaded and optionally linked to a project, propert=
y,
sale, rental, or customer record, using the FileStorageService to manage
physical storage under wwwroot/uploads. The Notifications module runs a
background check, executed on every dashboard load through
INotificationService.GenerateSystemNotificationsAsync, which scans for pend=
ing
payments, contracts expiring within thirty days, and projects that have fal=
len
behind schedule, and raises an in-application notification, shown to the us=
er
through the notification-bell view component, for each condition detected. =
The
Reports module, implemented through IExportService, aggregates data across =
the
other modules to produce sales, revenue, property, customer, payment,
project-progress, and rental reports, each exportable to PDF using QuestPDF=
 or
to Excel using ClosedXML. Finally, the Users module, restricted to the
Administrator role, allows new staff accounts to be created and assigned to=
 one
of the five roles defined in Models/Roles.cs.</p>

<h1><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-font-kerni=
ng:
18.0pt'>Chapter Five: System Implementation and Testing<o:p></o:p></span></=
h1>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>5.1 Introduct=
ion<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>This chapter presents the outcome of implementing the design described in
Chapter Four, and documents the testing activities carried out to verify th=
at
the resulting system behaves correctly. The system was implemented
incrementally, module by module, following the iterative cycle described in
Section 3.5, and each module was exercised manually using the demonstration
accounts and seed data generated automatically by the application's
DbInitializer component on first run. Screenshots of the running system,
captured directly from the deployed application, are presented in Section 5=
.3
to illustrate the completed interface.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>5.2 Testing<o=
:p></o:p></span></h2>

<h3><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>5.2.1 Unit-le=
vel
verification<o:p></o:p></span></h3>

<p>Because the correctness of the system depends heavily on a small number =
of
business rules rather than on complex algorithms, testing focused on direct=
ly
verifying those rules through the service layer described in Section 4.6. T=
able
5.1 summarises representative test cases exercised against the SalesService,
RentalService, and PaymentService classes, each of which was confirmed to
behave as expected.</p>

