<h1><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman";mso-font-kerni=
ng:
18.0pt'>Chapter Six: Conclusion and Recommendations<o:p></o:p></span></h1>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>6.1 Conclusio=
n<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>This study set out to design, develop, and evaluate a web-based Real Est=
ate
Project Management System capable of replacing the manual and spreadsheet-b=
ased
methods that many small real estate companies currently depend on. The
completed system, built using ASP.NET Core 9, Entity Framework Core 9, and
Microsoft SQL Server, integrates project tracking, property inventory, cust=
omer
records, sales and rental transactions, payment processing, document storag=
e,
reporting, and system-generated notifications into a single, role-aware web
application. Testing confirmed that the core business rules identified duri=
ng
requirements analysis, including automatic property status updates and
automatic payment balance recalculation, function correctly, and that each =
of
the five defined user roles can complete its intended tasks. The study
therefore concludes that its general and specific objectives, stated in Sec=
tion
1.3, were achieved.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>6.2 Summary of
findings<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>The requirements analysis confirmed that the central weaknesses of manual
and spreadsheet-based real estate record keeping are outdated property stat=
us
information, error-prone manual balance calculation, the absence of automat=
ic
warnings for overdue payments or expiring contracts, and scattered document
storage, as discussed in Section 1.2. The system design and implementation
described in Chapters Three and Four directly addressed each of these weakn=
esses:
property status is now updated automatically whenever a sale or rental is
created or removed, payment balances are recalculated automatically after e=
very
payment transaction, notifications are generated automatically for pending
payments, expiring contracts, and delayed projects, and documents are stored
centrally and linked to their related records. The testing results presente=
d in
Chapter Five confirmed that these mechanisms operate correctly across the
demonstration data used for evaluation.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>6.3 Recommend=
ations
and suggestions<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Based on the findings summarised above, it is recommended that any real
estate company considering adoption of a system of this kind first document=
 its
own specific business rules and approval workflows before configuring the s=
oftware,
since even a well-designed generic system cannot anticipate every local
business practice. It is further recommended that staff receive brief,
structured training on the system before its introduction, particularly
regarding the payment recording workflow, since the accuracy of the
automatically calculated balances depends entirely on every payment being
entered promptly and completely. Finally, it is recommended that a company
adopting this system perform regular database backups, since, unlike a
collection of paper files, the entire operational record of the company wou=
ld
reside in a single database.</p>

<h2><span style=3D'mso-fareast-font-family:"Times New Roman"'>6.4 Future
enhancement<o:p></o:p></span></h2>

<p>Several directions for future enhancement were identified but fall outsi=
de
the scope defined in Section 1.4. A dedicated mobile application, or a
responsive customer-facing portal, would allow customers to review their
payment status without needing to contact staff directly. Integration with
short message service and electronic mail providers would allow the existing
in-application notifications described in Section 4.6 to also reach staff a=
nd
customers outside the application itself. Integration with mobile money and
banking application programming interfaces would allow payments to be recor=
ded
automatically rather than manually. Finally, extending the system to support
multiple branches or multiple independent companies within a single deploym=
ent,
using a tenant identifier added to the core entities described in Section 4=
.5,
would broaden the system's applicability beyond the single-organisation sco=
pe
adopted in this study.</p>

